    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The housing and banking sectors are rife with systemic racism, perpetuated by policies and practices that disproportionately affect people of color. Redlining, for instance, was a deliberate practice where banks refused loans to people in predominantly Black neighborhoods, effectively segregating communities and limiting wealth accumulation. Banks also offered predatory loans to minority borrowers, trapping them in cycles of debt and foreclosure. These mechanisms ensure that racial disparities in wealth and housing persist, maintaining a racially stratified society. It's crucial to understand these dynamics to address and dismantle systemic racism.",
